// frontseat.workspace.v1alpha1: workspaces. A workspace is a working area
// of repositories joined into one graph, for a person, an agent or a job.
// It lives in a sandbox, which is its isolation and knows nothing of it,
// and a supervisor there holds its source: one workspace and one
// supervisor in a sandbox, which this API has no way to say otherwise.
//
// The control plane alone serves this service, and is the one writer of
// what a workspace is asked for: it makes a sandbox for a new one, tells
// the supervisor there what to hold and reads back what it holds, and
// ends one. It also forwards connections to each workspace's shell and
// to a port of its sandbox, by the workspace's name, and forwards
// nothing else to a workspace. A workspace is changed from outside it:
// inside its sandbox its source is fixed. A workspace is built from
// inside it: the build API (frontseat.build.v1alpha1) is served there,
// by a build daemon the command line starts in the sandbox, on its
// loopback, and the control plane neither serves that API nor forwards
// a call of it.
//
// Every call to a control plane is a person's, known by the token they
// present as a bearer token. A call that presents none is refused
// UNAUTHENTICATED with the reason IDENTITY_MISSING, and one whose token
// the control plane does not know with IDENTITY_INVALID, each in
// ErrorInfo of the control plane's own domain, frontseat.dev, in which
// every service it serves refuses a caller it does not know; a
// connection to a workspace's shell or to a port of its sandbox is held
// to the same. A workspace belongs
// to its owner, the caller who asked for it first. Its name is unique
// among all the workspaces of a control plane, whoever owns them. A
// caller who names a workspace
// that is another's is refused PERMISSION_DENIED with the reason
// NOT_OWNER, by every call of this service, and no caller is listed
// another's workspaces. What this service refuses by name carries ErrorInfo of
// the domain workspace.frontseat.dev, with the reason each call names.
//
// A connection to a port of a workspace's sandbox is asked for at
// /workspaces/{name}/ports/{port} of the control plane's address, as a
// WebSocket, and to its shell at /workspaces/{name}/shell. Each is the
// workspace's owner's alone, and anyone else is refused NOT_OWNER as
// above. What is written to the WebSocket, in binary messages, is what
// is connected to the port inside the sandbox reads, and what that
// writes is read from it: the control plane carries the bytes and knows
// nothing of what speaks there. The connection ends, both ways, when
// either end does. {port} is the port's number, from 1 to 65535, in
// digits alone. A connection is carried to any port but one, the port
// of the supervisor's internal source API, which is the control plane's
// own: that port, and whatever is written for {port} that is no port
// so written, is refused UNIMPLEMENTED with the reason PORT_NOT_SERVED,
// as a call is refused, and nothing is asked of the sandbox. A refusal
// is answered in place of the WebSocket, as the Connect protocol writes
// a unary call's error. A workspace that is in no sandbox yet is
// refused FAILED_PRECONDITION with the reason WORKSPACE_NOT_READY. A
// port that is not reached, as one nothing in the sandbox listens on,
// is refused FAILED_PRECONDITION with the reason PORT_UNREACHED, and
// nothing more is said of why. A workspace is carried 64 connections at
// once, its shells among them: one more is refused RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED
// with the reason TUNNELS_EXHAUSTED, until one of them has ended.
//
// A workspace is local or remote. A local one, which every control plane
// serves, clones its repositories in its sandbox: what is done there is
// committed there, and leaves it by a push. A remote one holds its
// repositories from a snapshot store, mounted in its sandbox and not
// cloned (WorkspaceSpec.stored, task and land). A remote workspace is
// served only by a control plane that was given what serves stored
// repositories, which an enterprise server is: any other refuses a spec
// that asks for one UNIMPLEMENTED, with the reason STORE_NOT_SERVED,
// and makes nothing.
//
// A workspace is kept as what was asked for, its spec, and what is so,
// its status. A call writes the spec and returns; the control plane then
// works until the status matches it. It works from the spec it keeps: a
// control plane or a supervisor that was stopped and started again carries
